The body AB Connect returns for a lookup of a standard GUID that does not exist.

VERBATIM from a live probe, gate G-5 result in 01-sdk-v3.md section 12, run 2026-08-02 against devel
partner sws:

  a lookup of a nonexistent standard GUID returned
  404 {"errors":[{"status":"404","title":"Not Found","detail":null}]}

The file is that body, byte for byte, on one line as the service sent it.

Documented shape:
  https://developerdocs.instructure.com/services/ab-connect/introduction/error-responses.md
  01-sdk-v3.md section 5.2, which maps 404 to ABConnectNotFoundException.

The point of this fixture is the EXPLICIT NULL detail. AB Connect sends "detail": null rather than
omitting the member, and section 5.2's remark that "every member of ABConnectApiError is optional"
rests on exactly this observation. What a test must assert:
  - ParseErrors returns one error, not zero. A parser that rejected the object because a member was
    null would silently produce an empty Errors list and an exception message with nothing in it.
  - Detail is null. It must not become the string "null", and it must not become "".
  - Status is the string "404" and Title is "Not Found".
  - SourcePointer and SourceParameter are null; there is no "source" member.
  - A 404 response carrying this body maps to ABConnectNotFoundException.

Contrast with GetStandardAsync's documented behavior, which the same G-5 run also confirmed: a direct
GET /standards/{guid} for a GUID whose status is deleted returns HTTP 200 with the standard, no status
filter needed (see standard-lookup.json). A 404 therefore means the GUID is genuinely unknown, not
that it was retired, and the two cases must never be conflated.
